WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.465
Amazon-Style Marketplace, How the Whole System Works.

2
00:00:03.745 --> 00:00:08.886
Chapter I01b, from the Industry Designs part of the System Design Playbook.

3
00:00:10.636 --> 00:00:11.596
At a glance:

4
00:00:11.876 --> 00:00:13.177
What this page adds.

5
00:00:13.457 --> 00:00:17.660
I01 focuses on four hard problems with runnable labs.

6
00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.799
This page is the system walkthrough, with no code.

7
00:00:21.079 --> 00:00:41.601
It follows one order from search to doorstep and the seller's payout, and explains each subsystem: catalog, search, recommendations, pricing and the buy box, cart, checkout and payments;. the order pipeline, fulfilment centres, transportation and delivery, returns;. the seller side and advertising;. the algorithms behind them;.

8
00:00:42.951 --> 00:00:50.226
the data platform and warehouse;. the tech stack, with what has been publicly described kept separate from a reference choice.

9
00:00:51.576 --> 00:00:52.323
Warning:

10
00:00:52.603 --> 00:00:54.011
Sources and honesty.

11
00:00:54.291 --> 00:01:04.573
Everything attributed to Amazon comes from public sources: papers such as Dynamo (2007) and item-to-item collaborative filtering (2003);.

12
00:01:04.853 --> 00:01:11.253
AWS blog posts, the AWS Builders' Library, re:Invent talks and news coverage.

13
00:01:11.533 --> 00:01:12.984
These are marked VERIFY.

14
00:01:13.264 --> 00:01:22.629
Internal details are not public, so where this page says a system like this would... it is describing a reference design, not Amazon's implementation.

15
00:01:23.979 --> 00:01:24.833
Part 1.

16
00:01:25.113 --> 00:01:27.886
The Business in Numbers (orders of magnitude).

17
00:01:29.136 --> 00:01:30.501
Here is a quick table:

18
00:01:30.781 --> 00:01:34.344
Quantity, and Order of magnitude (VERIFY current figures).

19
00:01:34.624 --> 00:01:36.288
Products (catalog entries):

20
00:01:36.568 --> 00:01:37.805
Hundreds of millions.

21
00:01:38.085 --> 00:01:38.896
Sellers:

22
00:01:39.176 --> 00:01:44.147
Millions, with the majority of units sold by third-party sellers (VERIFY share).

23
00:01:44.427 --> 00:01:45.323
Customers:

24
00:01:45.603 --> 00:01:46.840
Hundreds of millions.

25
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.912
Fulfilment and delivery sites:

26
00:01:49.192 --> 00:01:54.397
Hundreds of fulfilment centres, plus sortation centres and delivery stations worldwide.

27
00:01:54.677 --> 00:01:55.616
Peak events:

28
00:01:55.896 --> 00:02:04.707
Prime Day and holiday peaks, with AWS publishing per-service peak metrics for the retail business in yearly blog posts (VERIFY).

29
00:02:06.057 --> 00:02:07.443
What this scale implies:

30
00:02:07.723 --> 00:02:10.774
Everything is sharded by customer, product or seller.

31
00:02:11.054 --> 00:02:14.446
Everything is owned by a small team behind an API.

32
00:02:14.726 --> 00:02:15.985
Everything is measured.

33
00:02:17.335 --> 00:02:18.209
Part 2.

34
00:02:18.489 --> 00:02:20.068
The Architecture in Layers.

35
00:02:21.318 --> 00:02:22.662
Here is the diagram.

36
00:02:26.512 --> 00:02:30.011
Organising principles (publicly described; VERIFY):

37
00:02:30.291 --> 00:02:32.061
Service-oriented from early on.

38
00:02:32.341 --> 00:02:35.413
Teams own services end to end, including on-call.

39
00:02:35.693 --> 00:02:39.811
They communicate only through APIs, with no shared databases.

40
00:02:40.091 --> 00:02:43.248
Small teams, often described as two-pizza teams.

41
00:02:43.528 --> 00:02:48.285
Cells, shuffle sharding and constant-work designs for blast-radius control.

42
00:02:48.565 --> 00:02:54.944
Deployments roll out in waves, region by region and cell by cell (AWS Builders' Library).

43
00:02:55.224 --> 00:03:03.352
Correction of Errors (COE) documents after incidents, a blameless postmortem culture with root-cause analysis (VERIFY).

44
00:03:04.702 --> 00:03:05.598
Part 3.

45
00:03:05.878 --> 00:03:07.371
One Order, End to End.

46
00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:09.389
Search.

47
00:03:09.669 --> 00:03:12.400
Request: a customer types running shoes men.

48
00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:16.648
The request reaches a front-end fleet in their region, routed to a cell.

49
00:03:16.928 --> 00:03:30.240
Query understanding: spelling correction;. tokenisation and normalisation;. category and attribute prediction (department = shoes, gender = men, activity = running);. brand detection;.

50
00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:38.715
synonyms;. intent classification: navigational (nike air zoom) versus exploratory (gift for runner).

51
00:03:38.995 --> 00:03:48.361
Candidate retrieval: lexical retrieval over an inverted index of product text (titles, bullets, attributes, backend keywords sellers provide);.

52
00:03:49.711 --> 00:04:01.060
semantic retrieval with neural embeddings (query and product encoded into the same vector space; approximate nearest-neighbour search) to catch products that don't share words with the query.

53
00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:04.732
Amazon published semantic product search work (VERIFY:

54
00:04:05.012 --> 00:04:14.591
Semantic Product Search, KDD 2019);. filters: availability in the customer's region, and deliverability to the customer's address.

55
00:04:14.871 --> 00:04:17.836
Ranking: a learning-to-rank model scores candidates.

56
00:04:18.116 --> 00:04:18.991
Features:

57
00:04:20.341 --> 00:04:31.925
text relevance, and behavioural signals (clicks, add-to-carts, purchases for this query);. price, delivery speed, reviews and ratings, availability, seller quality;. personalisation.

58
00:04:32.205 --> 00:04:36.557
It is trained on historical searches with purchase and engagement labels.

59
00:04:36.837 --> 00:04:42.234
Ranking is multi-objective: relevance, conversion, customer experience signals.

60
00:04:42.514 --> 00:04:50.514
Blending: sponsored products (from the ads auction, §3.12) and widgets are blended into the results with limits.

61
00:04:51.864 --> 00:05:00.312
Rendering: the page aggregator combines results with prices and delivery promises (from the offer and promise services), then renders.

62
00:05:00.592 --> 00:05:04.133
The whole flow has a latency budget of a few hundred ms.

63
00:05:05.483 --> 00:05:06.977
Product detail page.

64
00:05:07.257 --> 00:05:26.905
When the customer clicks a product, the detail page aggregator calls tens of services in parallel with deadlines: catalog (title, images, attributes, variations);. offer listing and buy box (the winning offer);. price and deals;. delivery promise for the customer's address;. stock status;. reviews summary;.

65
00:05:28.255 --> 00:05:36.895
Q and A;. recommendations (frequently bought together, customers also viewed);. ads;. the customer's own state (bought before, in cart).

66
00:05:37.175 --> 00:05:41.847
Critical widgets (price, buy box, availability, Add to Cart) must render.

67
00:05:42.127 --> 00:05:46.201
Optional widgets degrade (see I01 §2.6).

68
00:05:47.551 --> 00:05:49.685
Catalog, one product, many sellers.

69
00:05:49.965 --> 00:05:53.378
Identity: each product has an identifier (ASIN).

70
00:05:53.658 --> 00:06:01.871
Sellers either match their offer to an existing ASIN (using UPC or EAN or ISBN or matching) or create one.

71
00:06:02.151 --> 00:06:10.066
Contributions: many sellers and vendors contribute data about the same product, which conflicts (different titles, wrong weights).

72
00:06:10.346 --> 00:06:18.986
An arbitration process picks the displayed value per attribute, using contributor trust, consistency checks and ML-based quality models.

73
00:06:19.266 --> 00:06:22.679
Duplicate detection merges accidental duplicates.

74
00:06:24.029 --> 00:06:31.688
Product types and schemas: attributes are defined per product type (shoes have size and width; TVs have screen size).

75
00:06:31.968 --> 00:06:34.272
Listings are validated against the schema.

76
00:06:34.552 --> 00:06:40.227
Variations: parent, child relationships (size and colour) group variants on one page.

77
00:06:40.507 --> 00:06:48.229
Storage: a versioned, attribute-level store, with change events feeding search indexing, the detail page and the data lake.

78
00:06:49.579 --> 00:06:51.627
Offers, pricing and the buy box.

79
00:06:51.907 --> 00:07:01.443
An offer = seller + ASIN + condition + price + shipping + fulfilment channel (seller-fulfilled or platform-fulfilled) + quantity.

80
00:07:01.723 --> 00:07:21.585
Pricing: sellers set prices, often through automated repricers;. the platform's own retail pricing uses automated competitive pricing (monitoring prices elsewhere, cost, demand elasticity; VERIFY specifics);. fair-pricing policies block unusually high prices (VERIFY policy details).

81
00:07:22.935 --> 00:07:26.753
The buy box: one offer wins the default Add to Cart position.

82
00:07:27.033 --> 00:07:38.447
The factors publicly described to sellers include landed price, fulfilment method and delivery speed, seller performance metrics and stock (VERIFY; the exact formula is not public).

83
00:07:38.727 --> 00:07:43.377
I01 L2 shows how to maintain it consistently under repricing.

84
00:07:44.727 --> 00:07:45.453
Cart.

85
00:07:45.733 --> 00:07:56.229
Always writable: the cart is the original use case of the Dynamo paper's highly available key-value design (VERIFY; today's implementation isn't public).

86
00:07:56.509 --> 00:08:01.693
Contents: item references and quantities, not prices, which are re-read at checkout.

87
00:08:01.973 --> 00:08:04.682
Merge semantics: see I01 L1.

88
00:08:04.962 --> 00:08:07.010
Saved for later is a separate list.

89
00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:09.999
Guest carts are merged into the account at sign-in.

90
00:08:11.349 --> 00:08:12.245
Checkout.

91
00:08:12.525 --> 00:08:14.275
Address and delivery options.

92
00:08:14.555 --> 00:08:23.237
The promise service computes delivery dates per shipping speed from inventory positions, carrier cut-offs and capacity (§3.9).

93
00:08:23.517 --> 00:08:30.216
Payment selection: cards (tokenised), wallets, gift balances, instalments and local methods per country.

94
00:08:30.496 --> 00:08:38.091
Order total: item prices re-validated;. promotions and coupons applied (a rules engine; Ch 49);.

95
00:08:39.441 --> 00:08:45.499
tax computed per jurisdiction by a tax engine using product tax codes and the ship-to address.

96
00:08:45.779 --> 00:08:55.657
Place order: a risk check (fraud score);. payment authorisation;. order creation with the promise and fulfilment plan attached;. confirmation to the customer.

97
00:08:57.007 --> 00:09:03.279
Payment is usually captured later, when items ship (common e-commerce practice; VERIFY per market).

98
00:09:04.629 --> 00:09:05.887
Payments and fraud.

99
00:09:06.167 --> 00:09:22.146
Payments: an authorisation at order time, and capture at shipment (partial captures for split shipments);. refunds on returns;. card data kept out of most systems through tokenisation (PCI DSS scope reduction; Ch 45).

100
00:09:22.426 --> 00:09:29.893
Fraud detection: real-time models score each order, using: account age and history, device and network signals;.

101
00:09:31.243 --> 00:09:35.893
address risk, payment instrument risk, velocity;. graph links between accounts.

102
00:09:36.173 --> 00:09:41.059
The actions are: accept, hold for review, step-up verification, or cancel.

103
00:09:41.339 --> 00:09:51.664
Amazon offers related capability as an AWS service, Amazon Fraud Detector (VERIFY that it is still offered), which reflects the kind of models used.

104
00:09:51.944 --> 00:10:01.821
Seller-side risk: new-seller verification, suspicious listing detection, counterfeit reports and brand registry protections (VERIFY programme names).

105
00:10:03.171 --> 00:10:04.387
The order pipeline.

106
00:10:04.667 --> 00:10:15.654
A long-running workflow: placed, then payment authorised, then released to fulfilment, then picked, then packed, then shipped (capture), then delivered, then return window, then closed.

107
00:10:15.934 --> 00:10:18.067
Each shipment is its own sub-workflow.

108
00:10:18.347 --> 00:10:22.145
Cancellation is possible until release to the fulfilment centre.

109
00:10:22.425 --> 00:10:26.371
Orchestrated by workflow engines with durable state and timers.

110
00:10:26.651 --> 00:10:38.363
The public analogue is AWS Step Functions and the Simple Workflow Service (SWF), whose paradigm came from internal use (VERIFY) (Ch 13, Ch 50).

111
00:10:39.713 --> 00:10:44.748
Events from every step go to tracking pages, notifications, analytics and finance.

112
00:10:46.098 --> 00:10:48.253
Inventory placement and the promise.

113
00:10:48.533 --> 00:10:54.293
Demand forecasting per product, per region, per day, as probabilistic (quantile) forecasts.

114
00:10:54.573 --> 00:11:06.199
Amazon researchers published deep-learning forecasting methods such as DeepAR and multi-horizon quantile recurrent forecasters (MQ-RNN) (VERIFY papers and use).

115
00:11:06.479 --> 00:11:18.085
Buying and placement: how much to buy, from forecasts, lead times and cost of stock-outs versus holding;. where to place it (which fulfilment centres), so most customers are near stock.

116
00:11:19.435 --> 00:11:23.104
I01 L3 shows placement dominates the promise.

117
00:11:23.384 --> 00:11:38.275
Inbound: vendors ship to the platform, and sellers using the platform's fulfilment (FBA) send inbound shipments;. the system chooses the destination FCs, and inventory is distributed later between FCs as needed.

118
00:11:38.555 --> 00:11:47.301
The promise: for a customer and item, find FCs with available stock, pick and ship cut-off times, carrier transit time and capacity.

119
00:11:47.581 --> 00:11:51.059
The fastest feasible option is shown as Arrives Tuesday.

120
00:11:52.409 --> 00:11:54.009
Inside a fulfilment centre.

121
00:11:54.289 --> 00:11:56.913
Receive: inbound units are scanned and checked.

122
00:11:57.193 --> 00:12:02.227
Stow: units are placed into any available bin, not a fixed location per product.

123
00:12:02.507 --> 00:12:06.411
This is random (chaotic) stow, widely described (VERIFY).

124
00:12:06.691 --> 00:12:11.769
It maximises space use and spreads popular items so many pickers can reach them.

125
00:12:12.049 --> 00:12:14.886
The system records exactly which bin holds what.

126
00:12:15.166 --> 00:12:15.849
Pick:

127
00:12:16.129 --> 00:12:20.459
Traditional FCs: pickers follow optimised pick paths through aisles.

128
00:12:21.809 --> 00:12:33.564
Robotic FCs: drive units carry shelving pods to picker stations (from the Kiva Systems acquisition in 2012, now Amazon Robotics; VERIFY current systems).

129
00:12:33.844 --> 00:12:37.449
Pick lists are batched by wave and by the chosen shipping cut-off.

130
00:12:37.729 --> 00:12:47.159
Pack: the system recommends a box size from item dimensions (cartonisation, a 3D bin-packing heuristic), or uses automated packaging.

131
00:12:47.439 --> 00:12:56.015
Label and sort: a label is applied, and the parcel is sorted to the right outbound lane: carrier and destination sortation centre.

132
00:12:57.365 --> 00:13:02.122
Throughput control: the warehouse management system balances work across stations.

133
00:13:02.402 --> 00:13:05.389
Labour planning uses forecasts of units per hour.

134
00:13:06.739 --> 00:13:08.659
Transportation and last mile.

135
00:13:08.939 --> 00:13:15.211
Middle mile: trucks and planes move parcels from FCs to sortation centres and delivery stations.

136
00:13:15.491 --> 00:13:19.181
Linehaul schedules are optimised against volume forecasts.

137
00:13:19.461 --> 00:13:33.413
Last mile: parcels are sorted by route at delivery stations;. route planning solves a vehicle routing problem with time windows (VRP-TW), with stop sequences, road network travel times and driver hours;.

138
00:13:34.763 --> 00:13:39.606
drivers include in-house partners and crowd-sourced drivers (VERIFY programmes).

139
00:13:39.886 --> 00:13:43.726
Tracking: scan events and driver app GPS update tracking.

140
00:13:44.006 --> 00:13:48.315
Delivery photo proof and OTPs are used for high-value items.

141
00:13:48.595 --> 00:13:51.198
Alternatives: lockers and pickup points.

142
00:13:52.548 --> 00:13:53.615
Advertising.

143
00:13:53.895 --> 00:13:57.820
Sponsored products: advertisers bid on keywords or targets.

144
00:13:58.100 --> 00:14:10.729
For each search, eligible ads go through an auction: the ranking combines bid times predicted click-through rate times relevance;. pricing is a second-price-like auction (VERIFY the current auction design).

145
00:14:11.009 --> 00:14:16.748
Budget pacing spreads spend across the day (Ch 49 L3 for pacing concepts).

146
00:14:17.028 --> 00:14:23.087
Measurement: attribution of purchases to ad clicks, and advertiser reporting from the data platform.

147
00:14:24.437 --> 00:14:25.269
Reviews.

148
00:14:25.549 --> 00:14:29.517
Collection: reviews and ratings, with Verified Purchase badges.

149
00:14:29.797 --> 00:14:30.991
Abuse detection:

150
00:14:31.271 --> 00:14:37.245
ML models, graph analysis of reviewer, seller relationships, and bulk suppression (VERIFY).

151
00:14:37.525 --> 00:14:41.493
Aggregated rating: a weighted model rather than a simple average.

152
00:14:41.773 --> 00:14:49.090
Amazon has said its star ratings use ML weighting by factors such as recency and verification (VERIFY).

153
00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:51.315
Returns.

154
00:14:51.595 --> 00:14:54.176
The return request is checked against policy.

155
00:14:54.456 --> 00:14:58.680
Options: drop-off, pickup, or refund at drop-off for some items.

156
00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:07.536
Grading: returned items are graded and routed to restock, sell as used or open-box, return to the seller, liquidate, or dispose.

157
00:15:07.816 --> 00:15:11.933
Money and fraud: refunds via payments, and return-abuse detection.

158
00:15:13.283 --> 00:15:14.435
The seller's side.

159
00:15:14.715 --> 00:15:19.921
Onboarding: identity and business verification, tax information and bank account.

160
00:15:20.201 --> 00:15:24.659
Listing: matching or creating ASINs, and bulk upload through APIs.

161
00:15:24.939 --> 00:15:27.905
Inventory: seller-fulfilled or platform-fulfilled.

162
00:15:28.185 --> 00:15:36.526
Orders: notifications and SLAs for seller-fulfilled orders, and performance metrics (late shipment, cancellation, defect rates).

163
00:15:36.806 --> 00:15:37.702
Settlement:

164
00:15:39.052 --> 00:15:55.201
a per-seller ledger: sales, less fees (referral, fulfilment, storage, ads), refunds and chargebacks;. held until the return window, then disbursed on a schedule, with statements;. double-entry accounting as in Ch 45 and I03.

165
00:15:56.551 --> 00:15:57.469
Part 4.

166
00:15:57.749 --> 00:15:59.199
Algorithms Catalog.

167
00:16:00.449 --> 00:16:01.815
Here is a quick table:

168
00:16:02.095 --> 00:16:04.548
Problem, and Algorithm (typical), and Where.

169
00:16:04.828 --> 00:16:07.111
Similar products (customers who bought).

170
00:16:07.391 --> 00:16:08.820
Algorithm (typical):

171
00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:22.924
Item-to-item collaborative filtering (publicly described, 2003): compute similarity between items from co-purchases, offline; online, look up similar items for what the customer bought or viewed.

172
00:16:23.204 --> 00:16:23.865
Where:

173
00:16:24.145 --> 00:16:25.319
Recommendations.

174
00:16:25.599 --> 00:16:26.985
Personalised ranking.

175
00:16:27.265 --> 00:16:28.695
Algorithm (typical):

176
00:16:28.975 --> 00:16:34.692
Learning-to-rank (gradient-boosted trees, neural rankers) on engagement and purchase labels.

177
00:16:34.972 --> 00:16:35.633
Where:

178
00:16:35.913 --> 00:16:37.001
Search, feeds.

179
00:16:37.281 --> 00:16:38.519
Semantic matching.

180
00:16:38.799 --> 00:16:40.228
Algorithm (typical):

181
00:16:40.508 --> 00:16:46.332
Two-tower neural embeddings + approximate nearest neighbour (HNSW or IVF).

182
00:16:46.612 --> 00:16:47.273
Where:

183
00:16:47.553 --> 00:16:48.705
Search retrieval.

184
00:16:48.985 --> 00:16:50.287
Query understanding.

185
00:16:50.567 --> 00:16:51.996
Algorithm (typical):

186
00:16:52.276 --> 00:16:57.183
Classifiers for category, attributes and intent; spell correction; synonyms.

187
00:16:57.463 --> 00:16:58.124
Where:

188
00:16:58.404 --> 00:16:59.172
Search.

189
00:17:00.522 --> 00:17:01.802
Demand forecasting.

190
00:17:02.082 --> 00:17:03.511
Algorithm (typical):

191
00:17:03.791 --> 00:17:06.863
Probabilistic deep-learning forecasters (quantiles).

192
00:17:07.143 --> 00:17:07.805
Where:

193
00:17:08.085 --> 00:17:09.706
Buying, placement, labour.

194
00:17:09.986 --> 00:17:11.287
Inventory placement.

195
00:17:11.567 --> 00:17:12.997
Algorithm (typical):

196
00:17:13.277 --> 00:17:18.738
Optimisation: minimise expected shipping cost and missed promises under capacity.

197
00:17:19.018 --> 00:17:19.679
Where:

198
00:17:19.959 --> 00:17:21.047
Supply chain.

199
00:17:21.327 --> 00:17:22.309
Buy box.

200
00:17:22.589 --> 00:17:24.018
Algorithm (typical):

201
00:17:24.298 --> 00:17:28.949
Scoring on landed price, delivery, seller performance (formula not public).

202
00:17:29.229 --> 00:17:29.890
Where:

203
00:17:30.170 --> 00:17:31.002
Offers.

204
00:17:31.282 --> 00:17:32.114
Pricing.

205
00:17:32.394 --> 00:17:33.823
Algorithm (typical):

206
00:17:34.103 --> 00:17:37.602
Competitive price monitoring, elasticity models, rules.

207
00:17:37.882 --> 00:17:38.543
Where:

208
00:17:38.823 --> 00:17:40.039
Retail pricing.

209
00:17:40.319 --> 00:17:41.493
Fulfilment plan.

210
00:17:41.773 --> 00:17:43.202
Algorithm (typical):

211
00:17:43.482 --> 00:17:47.514
Search over FC combinations, minimising cost under the promise.

212
00:17:47.794 --> 00:17:48.455
Where:

213
00:17:48.735 --> 00:17:51.061
Order release (I01 L3).

214
00:17:51.341 --> 00:17:52.279
Pick paths.

215
00:17:52.559 --> 00:17:53.989
Algorithm (typical):

216
00:17:54.269 --> 00:17:57.767
Heuristic TSP within zones, batching and wave planning.

217
00:17:58.047 --> 00:17:58.709
Where:

218
00:17:58.989 --> 00:18:00.226
Fulfilment centres.

219
00:18:01.576 --> 00:18:02.749
Box selection.

220
00:18:03.029 --> 00:18:04.459
Algorithm (typical):

221
00:18:04.739 --> 00:18:06.765
3D bin packing heuristics.

222
00:18:07.045 --> 00:18:07.707
