249
00:18:55.547 --> 00:18:57.787
Item-to-item collaborative filtering.

250
00:18:58.067 --> 00:19:02.803
A closer look at the algorithm from the published 2003 paper (VERIFY:

251
00:19:03.083 --> 00:19:06.283
Linden, Smith and York, IEEE Internet Computing):

252
00:19:06.563 --> 00:19:09.017
Offline, build an item, item table.

253
00:19:09.297 --> 00:19:11.985
For each item A, look at customers who bought A.

254
00:19:12.265 --> 00:19:21.950
For each other item B those customers bought, compute a similarity between A and B, for example, the cosine similarity of their customer-purchase vectors.

255
00:19:22.230 --> 00:19:24.641
Keep the top N similar items per item.

256
00:19:24.921 --> 00:19:31.449
Online, for a customer: take the items they bought, rated or viewed, and collect each one's similar items.

257
00:19:31.729 --> 00:19:35.633
Sum the similarity scores, remove items already bought, and rank.

258
00:19:36.983 --> 00:19:47.692
Why it scales: the expensive part is offline, and grows with items and co-purchase pairs, not with customers times items;. online, it is a few table lookups.

259
00:19:47.972 --> 00:19:56.484
What it teaches: precompute the heavy relationship offline, serve it cheaply online, and personalise by combining precomputed pieces.

260
00:19:56.764 --> 00:20:02.567
Modern systems add deep learning, sequence models and real-time signals on the same principle.

261
00:20:03.917 --> 00:20:04.877
Part 5.

262
00:20:05.157 --> 00:20:05.946
4A.

263
00:20:06.226 --> 00:20:07.250
Deep Dives.

264
00:20:08.500 --> 00:20:12.404
4A.1 From a seller's new listing to a searchable product.

265
00:20:12.684 --> 00:20:20.769
Submission: the seller submits a listing (via portal, feed file or API), which is validated against the product type schema.

266
00:20:21.049 --> 00:20:37.412
Matching: an identifier match (UPC or EAN or ISBN) links it to an existing product;. otherwise ML matching compares titles, attributes and images to detect duplicates;. if no match is found, a new product is created.

267
00:20:38.762 --> 00:20:42.495
Content arbitration chooses the displayed attribute values.

268
00:20:42.775 --> 00:20:49.687
Image and content quality checks run, and prohibited or restricted products are screened by rules and classifiers.

269
00:20:49.967 --> 00:21:02.554
Change events: the resulting catalog change events trigger: search document rebuild and partial index update;. embedding refresh (batch);. detail-page cache invalidation;. lake ingestion.

270
00:21:03.904 --> 00:21:14.251
Offer events: when the seller's offer (price, quantity) is created, events update the buy box (I01 L2) and the availability used for search filtering.

271
00:21:15.601 --> 00:21:19.121
4A.2 The recommendation pipeline in a modern form.

272
00:21:19.401 --> 00:21:32.243
Offline: item-to-item similarity tables (the 2003 approach) and embedding models trained on sequences of views and purchases;. candidate tables per item and per customer segment.

273
00:21:32.523 --> 00:21:41.291
Nearline: recent customer activity (last views, cart adds) is kept in a fast profile store and updated from streams within seconds.

274
00:21:41.571 --> 00:21:44.451
Online: gather candidates from several sources;.

275
00:21:45.801 --> 00:21:58.431
rank with a model using customer, item and context features;. apply business rules (availability, deliverability, diversity, no already-purchased consumables unless re-buy is likely).

276
00:21:58.711 --> 00:22:03.916
Placement: widget selection per page, with experiments on each widget's contribution.

277
00:22:05.266 --> 00:22:07.527
4A.3 How the promise stays true.

278
00:22:07.807 --> 00:22:17.642
Promise inputs: inventory positions (by FC), open capacity (by FC and shift), carrier cut-offs, and transit time distributions by lane.

279
00:22:17.922 --> 00:22:26.029
Using history: the service uses historical delivery performance by lane and day to pick a date it can meet with high probability.

280
00:22:26.309 --> 00:22:31.386
It is a quantile decision, not an average: a promise must be kept most of the time.

281
00:22:31.666 --> 00:22:37.319
When capacity tightens (peak season), promises lengthen automatically instead of being broken.

282
00:22:37.599 --> 00:22:41.077
After ordering: the order stores the promise it was sold with.

283
00:22:41.357 --> 00:22:45.005
Downstream systems prioritise orders at risk of missing it.

284
00:22:46.355 --> 00:22:48.616
4A.4 A peak-day request path.

285
00:22:48.896 --> 00:22:54.891
Front end: a request hits a cell's front-end fleet, and page aggregators fan out with deadlines.

286
00:22:55.171 --> 00:23:01.656
Degradation: under load, optional widgets are dropped first, and shedding protects checkout (Ch 18).

287
00:23:01.936 --> 00:23:05.648
Orders: they flow into the pipeline, where queues absorb bursts.

288
00:23:05.928 --> 00:23:08.979
Fulfilment release is paced by FC capacity.

289
00:23:09.259 --> 00:23:14.955
Monitoring: operations dashboards watch per-cell error rates, order rates and payment success.

290
00:23:15.235 --> 00:23:17.368
Deployments pause during peak windows.

